WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.370
- Hello.

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:03.640
Now, when you look at me right now,

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:08.560
you probably think that it's
me, Sal, talking to you.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.330
But really what is talking to you

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.760
is a society

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.510
of over 30 trillion cells

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:19.460
that have somehow
collectively convinced itself

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.060
that it is Sal.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.220
And what we're going to
study in these lessons

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.370
are exactly that,

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.370
that even though we think
we're one unified organism,

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.210
or frankly, all the
living things we see are,

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.500
we say oh, that's a
plant, and that's a frog,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.860
and that's a pigeon, and that's a Sal.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:37.110
It's not just a Sal.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.300
It's made up of trillions

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.810
and trillions of things called cells,

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.310
which as we'll learn,

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:45.380
they're the fundamental
building blocks of life.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.140
What makes them alive,

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:50.030
and if we were to go at
a even more basic scale,

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.070
all of a sudden things are not alive.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:54.890
It's a fascinating philosophical question.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:58.610
And you might think that
one thirty-trillionth of me

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.440
might be some kind of small, simple thing,

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.860
but as we'll see there's a
whole universe in each cell.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.480
Unimaginable complexity that scientists

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.310
are still trying to understand.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.870
In these lessons we're gonna talk about

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:14.560
the history of mankind trying to struggle

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.000
with the basic units of life.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.340
Is there a basic unit,

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.730
and how do we understand how it works,

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:22.560
also known as cell theory.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.760
We're going to learn about
the different types of cells

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.110
that even exist,

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:29.180
things like prokaryatic
and eukaryotic cells.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.820
Things like plant and animal cells,

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.400
what's similar, what's different?

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.820
And finally, we're gonna
learn about parts of the cell,

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.050
especially things like, say, the membrane,

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.280
which in and of itself is a fascinating

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.690
and complex piece of machinery.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:44.730
So, we're about to delve into
some of the deepest ideas

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.930
that mankind has always faced.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:48.430
What is life?

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:51.570
What makes one thing alive
and another thing not alive?

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.490
What is the most basic unit of life?

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:54.930
Where did it come from?

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.860
We're gonna touch on all of
those in the next few lessons.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:00.620
And I encourage, if you wanna dive deeper

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.060
and get more context,

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.550
and get practice to make sure

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.580
that you're understanding these concepts,

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.573
look at these lessons on khanacademy.org.

